      "id": "c9401ea4-ba4d-4313-876b-71cd5c1d1b82",
      "name": "Sticky Note5"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Cleanup and classification\nAfter the loop finishes, the done path goes to Deduplicate Keywords, which removes repeated suggestions and leaves a cleaner final keyword set; your screenshot shows 579 collected items reduced down to 37 unique items at that stage. Those 37 unique keywords are sent to Basic LLM Chain, which uses the connected Ollama Chat Model to classify each keyword into a JSON structure with fields like keyword, cluster, search_intent, and priority.\n\nBecause the LLM returns JSON as text, Code in JavaScript parses that text into proper n8n JSON objects so downstream nodes can use the fields directly. After parsing, the workflow sends those structured items to Append row in sheet, which writes one row per keyword into Google Sheets.",
        "height": 608,
        "width": 1008,
        "color": "#48CAE4"
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1136,
        -400
      ],
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "id": "ea2bd1b7-976b-454b-8a7c-992fae44d7a4",
      "name": "Sticky Note6"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "mode": "runOnceForEachItem",
        "jsCode": "const parsed = JSON.parse($json.text);\n\n// 1. Grab the country code from the very first node (Webhook)\n// Adjust 'Webhook' if your first node has a different name\nconst countryCode = $('Webhook').first().json.body.country_code || 'IN'; \n\n// 2. Format the current date based on that country code\nconst localDate = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(countryCode, {\n  year: 'numeric',\n  month: '2-digit',\n  day: '2-digit'\n}).format(new Date());\n\nreturn {\n  keyword: parsed.keyword,\n  cluster: parsed.cluster,\n  search_intent: parsed.search_intent,\n  priority: parsed.priority,\n  date: localDate // This sends the country-specific date to your sheet\n};"
      },
